When a dielectric sheet of thickness \(t = \frac{d}{3}\) and relative permittivity K is introduced, the capacitor can be considered as two capacitors in series. One capacitor \(C_1\) has vacuum as dielectric and thickness \(d - t = d - \frac{d}{3} = \frac{2d}{3}\). The other capacitor \(C_2\) has dielectric K and thickness \(t = \frac{d}{3}\).
The capacitance \(C_1\) (vacuum part) is:
C_1 = \frac{A \epsilon_0}{2d/3} = \frac{3A \epsilon_0}{2d}
Substituting \(C = \frac{A \epsilon_0}{d}\), we get:
C_1 = \frac{3}{2}C
The capacitance \(C_2\) (dielectric part) is:
